    },
    {
      "id": "block-adamw-intuition-note",
      "kind": "note",
      "level": "understanding",
      "locator": "§2, after Proposition 2",
      "text": "Plain-language model\n\nAdam changes the size of each coordinate’s loss-gradient update using its gradient history. If λθ is mixed into that gradient first, Adam also rescales the regularizer coordinate by coordinate. Parameters with different gradient histories therefore receive different effective shrinkage.\n\nAdamW keeps the jobs separate: Adam decides how to follow the data; weight decay decides how strongly to shrink the current parameters.",
      "createdAt": 1788253200000,
      "updatedAt": 1788771600000
    },
    {
      "id": "block-adamw-numeric-table",
      "kind": "table",
      "level": "applying",
      "locator": "Derived from §2, Algorithm 2",
      "title": "Toy scalar AdamW step",
      "columns": [
        "Part",
        "Expression",
        "Value",
        "Interpretation"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Starting parameter",
          "θ = 2.0",
          "2.000",
          "Value before the step"
        ],
        [
          "Adam direction",
          "α·m̂/(√v̂+ε)",
          "0.100",
          "Already normalized by Adam"
        ],
        [
          "Learning-rate multiplier",
          "η",
          "0.010",
          "Scales both terms in this example"
        ],
        [
          "Data update",
          "η × 0.100",
          "0.001",
          "Move requested by the loss"
        ],
        [
          "Decay update",
          "η × λ × θ; λ = 0.1",
          "0.002",
          "Direct shrinkage of the parameter"
        ],
        [
          "New parameter",
          "2.000 − 0.001 − 0.002",
          "1.997",
          "Data step and decay stay inspectable"
        ]
